1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,960
Sudden hearing loss, could loud headphones or unchecked blood pressure be putting your ears at risk?

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:06,800
This is a medical emergency.

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:12,240
Sudden sensorineural hearing loss needs treatment within 72 hours for the best chance of recovery.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:16,080
Here's what raises your risk, and the one mistake that makes it worse.

5
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:17,840
First, loud noise.

6
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:26,480
Frequent high-volume headphone use and loud venues like concerts damage the hair cells inside the cochlea, raising your risk of a sudden hearing drop.

7
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:29,040
Second, cardiovascular risk factors.

8
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:36,720
High blood pressure, diabetes, and high cholesterol are all linked to sudden hearing loss, since they restrict blood flow to the inner ear.

9
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:42,080
Beyond those risk factors, one habit makes the outcome far worse: waiting it out.

10
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:48,880
If one ear suddenly feels muffled, or you notice ringing or dizziness, brushing it off costs you time you can't get back.

11
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,920
Keep headphone volume moderate, and stay on top of blood pressure and blood sugar checkups.

12
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:58,800
Most importantly, see a doctor the same day if you suspect sudden hearing loss.

13
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,480
Earlier treatment means better odds of recovery, so don't wait.
